#--: Overview

#~"lib/system/tcc.z" compiles C #b:"in this process", through #~"libtcc".
It is what #~"zc --cc-mode inproc" uses instead of running a C compiler as
a child process, and it is available to any program that wants the same.

It buys the fast #b:"compile" and nothing else. The result is an executable
on disk, never a run in memory, so a program built this way still executes
as its own process: its exit code, its signals and its leaks are observed
exactly as they would be otherwise, and a program that crashes cannot take
its compiler down with it.

#p: #b:"Loaded, not linked"

#~"libtcc" is opened with #~"dlopen" at the point of use. tinycc is
LGPL-2.1 and this tree is dual MIT/Apache, so dynamic loading is what keeps
a binary built here out of the combined-work question; #~"make
static-tcc-guard" fails the build if a libtcc symbol reaches any binary,
rather than leaving the rule to discipline.

The loader is what needs #~"dl", and this unit says so:

#code language=zerolang: """
  require: unit {
      library: "dl"
  }
"""

A program that never mentions #~"tcc" therefore links no #~"dl" -- the link
line is written from the units a program actually reaches, not from a list
of everything the standard library might want.

#p: #b:"Members"

#ul:
  [#*: #> to=available: #b:"tcc.available" -- #~"{tccDir: StringView} out bool"]
  [#*: #> to=compiletoexe: #b:"tcc.compileToExe" -- #~"... out i32"]


#--: available

#code language=zerolang: """
  available: function {tccDir: StringView} out bool is native
"""

Can libtcc be loaded from #~"tccDir" and driven from here? Loads and
immediately unloads.

For diagnostics, and for a program deciding what to offer. #~"zc" does
#b:"not" ask this before building: #~"--cc-mode inproc" never falls back,
so it has nothing to decide, and asking would make a compile depend on a
probe.


#--: compileToExe

#code language=zerolang: """
  compileToExe: function {
      csrc:    StringView
      exe:     StringView
      tccDir:  StringView
      release: bool
      cflags:  StringView
      libs:    (List String).view
  } out i32 is native
"""

Compile #~"csrc" and link it to the executable #~"exe".

#~"tccDir" holds tcc's runtime payload -- #~"libtcc1.a", tcc's own headers,
and #~"libtcc.so" itself. tcc cannot find it alone: the path is fixed when
tcc is #b:"configured", so a tcc that has moved cannot resolve even
#~"stddef.h" until it is told. One directory answers every question, which
is why there is one parameter and not three.

#~"cflags" is parsed by tcc in the spelling it accepts on the command line.
#~"libs" are the #~"-l" names to link, the same ones the emitted C declares
on its own second line; #~"m" is added for you.

#p: #b:"Return"

#ul:
  [#*: #~"0" -- the executable was written.]
  [#*: #~"1" -- tcc rejected the program, or the link failed. Diagnostics
    have already gone to stderr, in the same words the #~"tcc" driver would
    have printed.]
  [#*: #~"127" -- libtcc could not be loaded. This is the code
    #~"os.spawn" already returns for "could not run the compiler", so a
    caller reports a missing compiler and an unloadable one through one
    path instead of growing a second.]
